{"name":"Snowdrop Explosion","hex":"#e0efe1","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"snowdrop","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the 20 species of the genus Galanthus of the Amaryllidaceae, bulbous flowering plants, bearing a solitary, pendulous, white, bell-shaped flower that appears, depending on species, between autumn and late winter or early spring, all native to temperate Eurasia.","Alternative letter-case form of Snowdrop (“a Royal Air Force police officer”)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To steal clothing (especially women's underwear) from a clothesline."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/snowdrop"},{"term":"explosion","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A violent release of energy (sometimes mechanical, nuclear, or chemical); an act or instance of exploding.","The sound of an explosion."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/explosion"}],"entries":[{"title":"Galanthus","blocks":[{"text":"Galanthus, or snowdrop, is a small genus of approximately 20 species of bulbous perennial herbaceous plants in the family Amaryllidaceae. The plants have two linear leaves and a single small white drooping bell-shaped flower with six petal-like (petaloid) tepals in two circles (whorls). The smaller inner petals have green markings."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Galanthus_nivalis.jpg/330px-Galanthus_nivalis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galanthus"},{"title":"Explosion","blocks":[{"text":"An explosion is a rapid expansion in volume of a given amount of matter associated with an extreme outward release of energy, usually with the generation of high temperatures and release of high-pressure gases. Explosions may also be generated by a slower expansion that would normally not be forceful, but is not allowed to expand, so that when whatever is containing the expansion is broken by the pressure that builds as the matter inside tries to expand, the matter expands forcefully. An example of this is a volcanic eruption created by the expansion of magma in a magma chamber as it rises to the surface. Supersonic explosions created by high explosives are known as detonations and travel through shock waves. Subsonic explosions are created by low explosives through a slower combustion process known as deflagration."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/A_detonation_erupts_as_U.S._Marines_with_the_1st_Explosive_Ordnance_Disposal_Company%2C_Combat_Logistics_Regiment_2_conduct_a_demolition_operation_in_Helmand_province%2C_Afghanistan%2C_March_17%2C_2013_130317-M-KS710-206.jpg/330px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Explosion"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Illustration_Galanthus_nivalis0.jpg/500px-Illustration_Galanthus_nivalis0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Illustration_Galanthus_nivalis0.jpg/1280px-Illustration_Galanthus_nivalis0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Illustration_Galanthus_nivalis0.jpg/500px-Illustration_Galanthus_nivalis0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Galanthus nivalis: Flora von Deutschland, Österreich und der Schweiz, 1885","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIllustration_Galanthus_nivalis0.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/At_Dunham_Massey_2024_014_-_Snowdrops.jpg/500px-At_Dunham_Massey_2024_014_-_Snowdrops.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/At_Dunham_Massey_2024_014_-_Sno